+# Mid-cycle plan changes
+
+Status: resolved
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Customers can't change their plan. Support cancels the subscription and resubscribes the customer by hand. That loses the subscription's history, charges a full new period on top of days already paid for, and depends on whoever does it getting the money right. Customers who want a better plan wait on support. Customers who want a cheaper plan, or to move between monthly and annual billing, have no clean way to do it.
+
+## Solution
+
+The billing package gains a single operation for a **plan change**: a customer moves their existing subscription to another plan, another interval, or both, without cancelling it. The rules (see `GLOSSARY.md` and ADR-0001) are:
+
+- An **upgrade** (a plan with a higher monthly list price) or a **lateral change** (a different plan with the same monthly list price) takes effect the day it is requested. It is billed by **proration** at the current interval's prices: a **proration credit** for the unused days of the old plan and a **proration charge** for the new plan over the same days. The period dates stay the same. An invoice is issued only when that nets to more than zero. Nothing is ever refunded.
+- A **downgrade** (a plan with a lower monthly list price) and an **interval switch** wait for the end of the current period as a **scheduled change**. Nothing is billed when they are requested. At the next renewal, the new period uses the scheduled terms and is billed at full price.
+- A request that combines an upgrade or lateral change with an interval switch is split. The plan part applies now and the interval switch is scheduled.
+- Each request states the full target terms and replaces whatever was scheduled before. Requesting the terms in effect now cancels a scheduled change.
+- During a free trial, every change applies immediately and nothing is billed.
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a customer, I want to move to a more expensive plan mid-period, so that I get its features straight away without waiting for my renewal.
+2. As a customer upgrading mid-period, I want to be charged only for the remaining days on the new plan, with credit for the days I already paid on the old one, so that I never pay twice for the same day.
+3. As a customer upgrading mid-period, I want my renewal date to stay the same, so that my billing calendar doesn't move.
+4. As a customer upgrading on the first day of my period, I want full credit for the old plan's price, so that I pay exactly the price difference.
+5. As a customer on an annual plan, I want an upgrade to be prorated at annual prices over the real number of days in my year (365 or 366), so that the amount is fair.
+6. As a customer, I want an invoice for my upgrade that shows the credit and the charge as separate lines, so that I can see how the amount was worked out.
+7. As a customer, I want to move to a cheaper plan and keep what I already paid for until my period ends, so that I'm not charged or refunded mid-period.
+8. As a customer who has scheduled a downgrade, I want my next renewal to bill the cheaper plan at its full price, so that the downgrade takes effect when I expected.
+9. As a customer, I want to move to a different plan with the same monthly price straight away, so that a sideways move doesn't wait for my renewal.
+10. As a customer making a lateral change whose price at my current interval is higher, I want to be billed the prorated difference, so that nobody under- or over-pays for the remaining days.
+11. As a customer whose change applies now but whose credit is at least the new charge, I want the change to go through with nothing billed, so that I'm never sent a negative invoice.
+12. As a monthly customer, I want to switch to annual billing from my next renewal, so that I start paying the annual price without any proration.
+13. As an annual customer, I want to switch to monthly billing from the end of my annual period, so that I keep the year I already paid for.
+14. As a customer who upgrades and switches interval in one request, I want the upgrade now and the interval switch at renewal, so that I get the better plan immediately and the new interval in the right place.
+15. As a customer who downgrades and switches interval in one request, I want both to wait for my renewal, so that nothing changes until my paid period ends.
+16. As a customer who changes my mind, I want to request my current plan and interval to cancel a scheduled change, so that I keep renewing exactly as before.
+17. As a customer, I want a new request to replace whatever I scheduled before, so that only my latest decision counts.
+18. As a customer who repeats a request already scheduled, I want nothing else to happen, so that a double-click does no harm.
+19. As a monthly customer who scheduled a switch to annual and then upgrades without naming an interval, I understand the upgrade replaces the scheduled switch and I stay monthly, so that each request means exactly what it says.
+20. As a customer on a free trial, I want to change plan or interval immediately with nothing billed, so that my first paid period uses the terms I picked.
+21. As a customer on a free trial, I want my trial end date to stay the same when I change plan, so that changing plan doesn't extend or shorten my trial.
+22. As a customer on a retired plan, I want to move to an active plan like anyone else, so that I'm not stuck on an old offering.
+23. As a customer on a retired plan with a scheduled change, I want to cancel that change by requesting my current terms, so that I keep my grandfathered plan.
+24. As a customer whose scheduled downgrade targets a plan that has since been retired, I want the downgrade honored at renewal, so that what I was promised when I asked is what happens.
+25. As a support agent, I want a request for a retired target plan to be rejected with the existing "plan not available" error, so that retired plans aren't sold again through plan changes.
+26. As a support agent, I want a request to switch interval while staying on a retired plan to be rejected with "plan not available", so that a grandfathered plan can renew but can't be bought again on new terms.
+27. As a support agent, I want a request for the plan and interval the customer already has, with nothing scheduled, to be rejected with a plan-change error, so that I notice a mistaken request.
+28. As a support agent, I want plan changes on a canceled subscription to be rejected with the existing "subscription not active" error, so that canceled customers must resubscribe.
+29. As a support agent, I want unknown subscriptions and unknown plans to raise the existing "not found" errors, so that plan changes behave like the rest of the API.
+30. As a support agent, I want a plan change on a subscription whose renewal is overdue (its period has ended but renewals haven't run yet) to be rejected with a plan-change error, so that I run renewals first and the proration is never based on a period that has already ended.
+31. As a support agent, I want to see on the subscription what change is scheduled and to which plan and interval, so that I can answer customers without waiting for the renewal.
+32. As a finance operator, I want every invoice from a plan change recorded in the store like any other invoice, dated the day of the change, so that reporting sees it.
+33. As a finance operator, I want each proration line rounded to whole cents half-up on its own, so that the invoice total is exactly the sum of its lines.
+34. As a finance operator, I want the plan-change operation never to produce a negative invoice or a refund, so that plan changes add no money-out path.
+35. As a finance operator, I want a renewal that catches up several missed periods to apply the scheduled change at the first of them only, so that later periods continue on the new terms and are billed correctly.
+36. As a developer calling the billing package, I want `change_plan` to return the invoice it issued, or `None` when nothing is billed at the moment of the request, so that I know whether to collect payment.
+37. As a developer, I want plan-change failures that no existing error describes to raise a new `InvalidPlanChange` error, a subclass of `BillingError`, so that I can catch them alongside other billing errors.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+**Public interface.**
+- `BillingService.change_plan(subscription_id: str, new_plan_code: str, new_interval: Interval | None = None) -> Invoice | None`. `new_interval=None` means the interval currently in effect, not the scheduled one.
+- New error `InvalidPlanChange(BillingError)` in the errors module, exported from the package like the other errors.
+
+**Scheduled change on the subscription.**
+- `Subscription` gains an optional `scheduled_change` field. It defaults to none, so existing construction keeps working.
+- The value is a small frozen value object, `ScheduledChange(plan_code, interval)`: the full plan and interval the subscription takes on at its next renewal. It covers a downgrade, an interval switch, or both. It is exported from the package.
+- At most one exists per subscription. It is only ever replaced or cleared as a whole.
+
+**Validation, in this order:**
+1. The subscription doesn't exist → `SubscriptionNotFound`. It's canceled → `SubscriptionNotActive`.
+2. The target plan doesn't exist → `PlanNotFound`.
+3. The renewal is overdue (`period_end <= today`, in either the trialing or active state) → `InvalidPlanChange`.
+4. The target plan is retired → `PlanNotAvailable`. The one exception is a request for the subscription's current plan at its current interval, which only confirms the terms in effect now and so isn't a sale. This means a request to stay on a retired plan but switch interval raises `PlanNotAvailable`.
+5. The target is the current plan at the current interval:
+   - with a scheduled change: clear it, save, return `None`, bill nothing;
+   - otherwise: `InvalidPlanChange`.
+
+**Trialing subscriptions.**
+- The plan code and interval are replaced right away.
+- The period (the trial) is unchanged and no scheduled change is set.
+- Nothing is billed and the call returns `None`.
+- The first paid period at trial end already uses the subscription's plan and interval.
+
+**Active subscriptions: classify the plan part**, comparing monthly list prices of the current plan and the target plan:
+- higher → upgrade;
+- equal, different plan → lateral change;
+- lower → downgrade;
+- same plan → no plan part.
+
+The interval part is an interval switch whenever the target interval differs from the current interval.
+
+**Outcome per combination.** The scheduled change after the request always *replaces* any earlier one.
+
+| Plan part | Same interval | Interval switch |
+|---|---|---|
+| Upgrade or lateral | Plan applies now, prorated. Scheduled change cleared. | Plan applies now, prorated. Scheduled change = (new plan, new interval). |
+| Downgrade | Scheduled change = (new plan, current interval). Returns `None`. | Scheduled change = (new plan, new interval). Returns `None`. |
+| Same plan | See validation step 5. | Scheduled change = (current plan, new interval). Returns `None`. |
+
+**Proration, for changes that apply now.**
+- `unused_days = period_end − today`; `period_days = period_end − period_start`. Both are calendar days, so today counts as a day on the new plan.
+- Prices are both plans' list prices at the subscription's **current** interval.
+- Proration credit = −(old price × unused_days / period_days), rounded to cents half-up.
+- Proration charge = new price × unused_days / period_days, rounded to cents half-up.
+- The subscription's plan code changes now. Interval, period start and period end stay the same.
+- If credit + charge > 0, an invoice dated today is issued through the same internal path as every other invoice (so it is recorded in the store), with the credit line followed by the charge line, and returned.
+- If the total is ≤ 0, no invoice is issued and the call returns `None`. The credit is forfeited and nothing is refunded (ADR-0001).
+- Line descriptions name the plan, the interval and the dates covered, in the style of the existing period lines. The credit line says it is a credit for unused time.
+
+Worked example with the existing test plans:
+- Basic is $10/month. A subscription starts 2026-01-15, so the period is [2026-01-15, 2026-02-15), 31 days.
+- On 2026-01-20 it upgrades to Pro ($30/month). There are 26 unused days.
+- Credit is −8.39 (10 × 26/31 = 8.387…). Charge is 25.16 (30 × 26/31 = 25.161…). The invoice total is 16.77.
+- The period still ends on 2026-02-15. The renewal then bills Pro at $30.00.
+
+**Renewals.**
+- When `renew_due` renews a subscription that has a scheduled change, the new period starts on the old `period_end`.
+- The period length follows the scheduled interval. The subscription takes on the scheduled plan and interval. The renewal invoice bills that plan's full price for that interval. The scheduled change is cleared.
+- The change applies even if its target plan has since been retired.
+- During a catch-up of several missed periods, only the first renewal applies the change. The rest continue on the new terms.
+
+**Cancel** is unchanged. A canceled subscription never renews, so any scheduled change on it simply never applies.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam, the existing one:** drive the public `BillingService` API with a `FixedClock` and `InMemoryStore`. Use `subscribe`, `renew_due` and `cancel` for setup and observation, and `change_plan` under test. Check results through:
+  - the returned `Invoice | None`;
+  - `store.get_subscription(...)`: plan code, interval, period dates, status, scheduled change;
+  - `store.invoices_for(...)`: line amounts, totals, issue dates.
+
+  No tests of private helpers or proration internals, and no store methods added just for tests.
+- **What makes a good test:** it states a customer-visible outcome (what is billed, when, and what the subscription looks like afterwards), using exact `Decimal` amounts and exact dates. Refactoring the internals should not break it.
+- **Prior art:** the existing service tests and their shared fixtures: the clock fixed on 2026-01-15, plus Basic ($10/month, $100/year) and Pro ($30/month, $300/year). Tests that need other prices add plans in the test itself, for example:
+  - an equal-monthly-price plan for lateral changes;
+  - a plan with a higher monthly but lower annual price for the zero-or-negative total case.
+- **Cases to cover:**
+  - Upgrade in the same interval:
+    - the monthly worked example;
+    - on the first day of the period (full credit);
+    - on an annual period of 366 days.
+  - Lateral change:
+    - equal prices at the current interval → `None`, plan changed;
+    - higher price at the current interval → invoice.
+  - An upgrade whose net is ≤ 0 → `None`, plan changed, no invoice recorded.
+  - A downgrade is scheduled and nothing is billed. At renewal the new period bills the downgraded plan.
+  - Interval switches:
+    - monthly → annual: scheduled; at renewal the period is 12 months, billed at the annual price;
+    - annual → monthly: the same in reverse.
+  - Upgrade plus interval switch: an invoice now, prorated at the current interval; the switch to the new plan's interval is scheduled.
+  - Downgrade plus interval switch: everything scheduled.
+  - Replacement rules:
+    - a new request replaces the scheduled change;
+    - an upgrade clears a scheduled change;
+    - an upgrade with `new_interval=None` drops a scheduled interval switch;
+    - repeating the scheduled request leaves the same scheduled change;
+    - requesting the current terms clears it and returns `None`.
+  - Trialing:
+    - plan and interval swapped immediately;
+    - trial end unchanged;
+    - nothing billed;
+    - the first paid period bills the new terms.
+  - Retired plans:
+    - a retired target → `PlanNotAvailable`;
+    - staying on a retired plan with an interval switch → `PlanNotAvailable`;
+    - moving off a retired plan to an active one works;
+    - clearing a scheduled change while on a retired plan works;
+    - a scheduled downgrade to a plan retired afterwards is honored at renewal.
+  - Catch-up renewal across several periods applies the scheduled change once, at the first renewal.
+  - Errors:
+    - unknown subscription;
+    - unknown plan;
+    - canceled subscription;
+    - same terms with nothing scheduled;
+    - overdue renewal (including the day of `period_end`, before renewals run).
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Customer credit balances, refunds, and invoices with a negative total.
+- Proration of interval switches, and moving the renewal date when the interval changes.
+- Previewing what a plan change would cost before making it.
+- Cancelling at period end, pausing subscriptions, changing the length of a trial.
+- Seats or quantities, coupons, taxes, payment collection, customer notifications.
+- Changing plan prices, and re-pricing existing subscriptions when a plan changes.
+- A database-backed store. The production store keeps the same interface and will need to persist the new scheduled-change field.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Vocabulary follows `GLOSSARY.md` (Plan change, Upgrade, Downgrade, Lateral change, Interval switch, Scheduled change, Proration, Proration credit, Proration charge). Rationale for never paying out and for deferring interval switches is in ADR-0001.
+- On the retired-plan exception in validation step 4: a customer on a retired plan who requests their current plan at their current interval only confirms the terms in effect now. This doesn't sell the plan, so it isn't rejected, and it can still cancel a scheduled change. Any other request targeting a retired plan is.
+- Choosing upgrade versus downgrade by *monthly* list price while prorating at the *current interval's* prices is deliberate. It is why an upgrade can net to zero or less on an annual subscription.
